From: Helix <https://github.com/Nibir1/Helix>
Subject: [PATCH] feat: condition generation on prior conversation turns

CSM's distinguishing capability is that its prosody is conditioned on how the
conversation has been going. The model card says it "sounds best when provided
with context", and the reference Python API takes prior turns as
Segment(text, speaker, audio). csm.rs currently tokenizes only the current
utterance, so a local CSM sounds like a very good single-shot voice rather than
a participant in a dialogue.

This adds optional context to both the library and the HTTP server.

Everything needed is already present: `audio_tokens_and_mask(frame)` turns one
Mimi frame into model tokens+mask, and `self.audio_tokenizer` is a `mimi::Mimi`
which encodes as well as decodes. Context is therefore token assembly, not new
model loading — no extra weights, no extra VRAM beyond the KV cache the longer
prompt occupies.

Design notes for review:

* Segments are interleaved text-then-audio, oldest first, exactly as the
  reference implementation orders them, and the current utterance's prompt is
  appended last.
* A segment with no audio is valid and useful: CSM conditions on text too, and a
  caller that streamed a turn may have the transcript without one contiguous clip.
* Audio is expected as 24 kHz mono f32 — the tokenizer's own sample rate, read
  from `audio_tokenizer.config().sample_rate` rather than assumed.
* Context is truncated from the FRONT to `max_seq_len` before generation, so a
  long history degrades to recent history instead of erroring.
* **`X-CSM-Context-Segments` is returned on every response.** Serde ignores
  unknown fields by default, so an unpatched server accepts a `context` field and
  silently drops it — a client cannot tell conditioning from no conditioning.
  The header makes support observable rather than assumed. This is the one part
  of the patch that exists for clients rather than for the model, and it is the
  part that stops "conversational" from being an unverifiable claim.

